From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 07:19:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: exynos9820-beyond2lte: Enable the USB
 port

The port is described as peripheral rather than dual role.  It is wired as
dual role and the phone changes roles with a USB-C controller sitting on a
companion chip, which nothing in this series reaches; a dual role port
without that controller waits for a role that never arrives.  Peripheral is
the half that works with a plain cable to a computer, and it is the half
this machine needs to be able to report anything about itself.

Neither the controller nor the PHY has its supplies named.  They come from
rails of the main PMIC, which sits behind a mailbox this series does not
speak to, and the regulator core substitutes its own dummy for a supply a
populated tree does not name.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820-beyond2lte.dts | 48 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 48 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820-beyond2lte.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820-beyond2lte.dts
index e78da54621f..00a421b3009 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820-beyond2lte.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820-beyond2lte.dts
@@ -362,6 +362,13 @@ ts_int: ts-int-pins {
 	};
 };
 
+/ {
+	reg_usb_dummy: regulator-usb-dummy {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "usb_dummy";
+	};
+};
+
 &serial0 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
@@ -370,6 +377,47 @@ &ufs_0 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * The port is described as peripheral only.  It is wired as dual role and
+ * the phone changes roles with a USB-C controller sitting on the max77705,
+ * which nothing in this series can reach; a dual role port without that
+ * controller waits for a role that never arrives.  Peripheral is the half
+ * that works with a plain cable to a PC, and it is the half this series
+ * needs to be able to report anything about itself.
+ *
+ * The supplies are named against a stand-in.  They really come from rails
+ * of the main PMIC, which sits behind the ACPM mailbox and which nothing
+ * here can reach, and the driver would be happy with the dummy the
+ * regulator core hands out for a supply nobody names.  The binding is not:
+ * it lists both as required, so they are named, and named honestly as what
+ * they are rather than as rails this series pretends to drive.
+ */
+&usbdrd {
+	status = "okay";
+	vdd10-supply = <&reg_usb_dummy>;
+	vdd33-supply = <&reg_usb_dummy>;
+};
+
+/*
+ * The port is wired to the only connector this phone has, so it has to work
+ * both ways: as a device, which is how the machine is reached from a computer,
+ * and as a host, so that a keyboard can be plugged into the phone.
+ *
+ * There is no Type-C driver here to sense which way the cable goes, so the
+ * role is switched by hand through the role switch dwc3 registers, and the
+ * default is spelled out: a board that came up as a host would drop the
+ * connection to the computer and leave no way back in.
+ */
+&usbdrd_dwc3 {
+	dr_mode = "otg";
+	usb-role-switch;
+	role-switch-default-mode = "peripheral";
+};
+
+&usbdrd_phy {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 /* This USI instance is wired as the touchscreen I2C master on this board. */
 &usi3 {
 	samsung,mode = <USI_V2_I2C>;
